C = [
("h1", "8. The Lord’s Answer About John"),
("p", "Claim: when the Lord speaks of John, he says three things that the volume holds side by side. John is “more than a prophet” and the messenger of Malachi 3:1. John is Elias “if ye will receive it.” And this generation refused both John and the Son of man. The first two are said in one sitting, and the third follows at once. The chapter reads Matthew 11 and Luke 7 in order, with the question that John asked from prison, the answer the Lord gave to John’s disciples, and the words the Lord then spoke to the crowds."),
("sub", "8.1 The question from prison"),
("p", "“Now when John had heard in the prison the works of Christ, he sent two of his disciples, and said unto him, Art thou he that should come, or do we look for another?” (Matthew 11:2, 3). Luke tells the same story at greater length, with the disciples repeating the question to the Lord (Luke 7:19, 20). The question is John’s, from prison, about the Lord. The words “he that should come” echo the language of the coming one, and the volume does not interpret John’s motive. The text gives the question and nothing of why it was asked."),
("p", "The Lord’s answer is a list of works: “Go and shew John again those things which ye do hear and see” (Matthew 11:4), and the works follow: “The blind receive their sight, and the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, and the deaf hear, the dead are raised up, and the poor have the gospel preached to them” (Matthew 11:5). Luke adds that in that same hour he “cured many of their infirmities and plagues, and of evil spirits” (Luke 7:21). The list is drawn from Isaiah’s pictures of the coming one: the blind seeing and the lame walking (Isaiah 35:5, 6) and good tidings to the poor (Isaiah 61:1). The answer to “art thou he” is to point to what Isaiah said would be done. Then a word to John: “And blessed is he, whosoever shall not be offended in me” (Matthew 11:6)."),
("sub", "8.2 More than a prophet"),
("p", "When the messengers have gone, the Lord turns to the multitudes. Three times he asks what they went out to see: “A reed shaken with the wind?” “A man clothed in soft raiment?” “A prophet?” (Matthew 11:7–9). The answer to the third question is “yea, I say unto you, and more than a prophet.” Then the text: “For this is he, of whom it is written, Behold, I send my messenger before thy face, which shall prepare thy way before thee” (Matthew 11:10). Luke’s form is “and much more than a prophet,” with the same quotation (Luke 7:26, 27)."),
("p", "The Lord’s application of Malachi 3:1 to John is complete and plain. The volume follows it. John is the messenger of whom Malachi wrote, and he prepared the way. In the sentence that follows the Lord says, “Among them that are born of women there hath not risen a greater than John the Baptist: notwithstanding he that is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he” (Matthew 11:11). The saying about “the least in the kingdom” and the saying that follows about violence, “from the days of John the Baptist until now the kingdom of heaven suffereth violence, and the violent take it by force” (Matthew 11:12), are read in Prophecy & Mystery Volume 3 and in the first volume of the Readers Question Series, and this volume cites them and does not argue them."),
("sub", "8.3 “If ye will receive it”"),
("p", "Then the sentences on which the whole question turns. “For all the prophets and the law prophesied until John. And if ye will receive it, this is Elias, which was for to come. He that hath ears to hear, let him hear” (Matthew 11:13–15). Luke’s account does not carry these sentences. The statement is conditional, “if ye will receive it,” and it is addressed to the multitudes. The clause “which was for to come” is in the past tense of expectation: Elias was the one who was to come. The Lord says that John is that Elias, on the condition that the hearers will receive it."),
("p", "The condition is not decoration. A statement made on the condition of a hearer’s reception is a statement about an offer. If the offer is received, John is Elias in the sense the Lord names. If it is not, the statement does not take effect in that sense, and the text will show in chapter 9 that the Lord speaks of Elias as one who “shall first come” after John has been put to death. The volume reads Matthew 11:14 as the Lord’s offer to the nation, made in John’s day, of the identification of John as the Elias of Malachi. It reads the offer as one the nation did not accept, and the next sentences confirm that."),
("sub", "8.4 This generation"),
("p", "The Lord goes on: “But whereunto shall I liken this generation? It is like unto children sitting in the markets, and calling unto their fellows, and saying, We have piped unto you, and ye have not danced; we have mourned unto you, and ye have not lamented. For John came neither eating nor drinking, and they say, He hath a devil. The Son of man came eating and drinking, and they say, Behold a man gluttonous, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners” (Matthew 11:16–19). The generation refused John for his austerity and the Son of man for his ease. Both messages were rejected."),
("p", "Luke adds a verse on the division within the people: “And all the people that heard him, and the publicans, justified God, being baptized with the baptism of John. But the Pharisees and lawyers rejected the counsel of God against themselves, being not baptized of him” (Luke 7:29, 30). The people and the publicans justified God, and the Pharisees and lawyers rejected his counsel. The Pharisees are the company from which the deputation of John 1:24 came. The Gospel record shows a split: some received John, and the leaders refused him."),
("sub", "8.5 Two words, one scene"),
("p", "The chapter has now two statements about John from the Lord within a few lines. John is the messenger of Malachi 3:1, and John is Elias if the hearers will receive it. They are not the same sentence and the Lord does not merge them. The first is a statement of fact grounded in a text, “this is he, of whom it is written.” The second is a conditional about the hearers. The volume holds them as the Lord states them. It does not say that John failed or that the Lord withdrew an offer. It says that the Lord made one, and that the generation he describes in verses 16–19 did not take it."),
("ctbl", "The Lord’s statements about John.", [
    ("Passage", "What the Lord says", "The form"),
    ("Matthew 11:9", "A prophet, “and more than a prophet”", "A statement"),
    ("Matthew 11:10; Luke 7:27", "“This is he, of whom it is written, Behold, I send my messenger”", "A text applied: Malachi 3:1"),
    ("Matthew 11:11; Luke 7:28", "None greater among those born of women; the least in the kingdom greater", "A comparison"),
    ("Matthew 11:14", "“If ye will receive it, this is Elias, which was for to come”", "A conditional offer"),
    ("Matthew 11:16–19; Luke 7:31–35", "This generation refused John and the Son of man", "A rebuke"),
    ("Luke 7:29, 30", "The people and publicans justified God; the Pharisees and lawyers rejected his counsel", "A division"),
  ], [0.22, 0.5, 0.28]),
("sub", "8.6 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "John, from prison, asks whether the Lord is he that should come, and the Lord answers with works drawn from Isaiah and a word of blessing to the one not offended.",
    "The Lord applies Malachi 3:1 to John without qualification: “this is he, of whom it is written.”",
    "Matthew 11:14 is conditional: “if ye will receive it, this is Elias, which was for to come.” It is addressed to the multitudes and is not in Luke’s account.",
    "The generation the Lord describes refused both John and the Son of man; Luke records the split between the people and the Pharisees and lawyers.",
    "The volume reads Matthew 11:14 as an offer made to Israel in John’s day and not taken up, and reads the Lord’s words in Matthew 17 in the next chapter.",
  ]),
]
